# Intent: The remote token gate on the other remote-facing tools (owner brief 2026-11-16). Until now only self.remote.say checked the one-use cue token from self.remote.action; a controller could still move the head, chirp a find, take a photo, start a dance or a game, or run the listening test with no cue. Now each of those tools takes two more arguments, source and token (head moves already had source). A call whose source is "remote" must carry the token the unit answered for that kind (move, find, photo, dance, game, listen), or it is ignored, logged in the remote history with its reason, and answers {ok:false, reason}. A call with any other source (none, "user", the unit's own) is local and unchanged, so Y01's source user stays as it is. A token is one use; the kind then stays open for the cue's banner seconds (set_window: the "seconds" of self.remote.action, 1 to 30) so a nod, a find loop or a game start that makes several calls needs one cue, and the window closes at once when the session ends or the dead-man timeout runs out. Head moves inside a find carry cue_kind find. Only a find chirp (find=1) is gated; ending a find (find=2) never is.
# Placement: unit (the gate runs on the robot with no host; the host marks the calls it makes for a paired controller)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/{maipai_remote_core.h,maipai_remote.h,maipai_remote.cc,hal_mcp.cpp,maipai_games.cc,maipai_sounds.cc,maipai_listen.cc} (ours, TB05, 020, 116, G01, TB01) and xiaozhi-esp32 main/mcp_server.cc (MIT, the camera tool)
# Check after a flash: self.robot.dance {"source":"remote"} answers {ok:false,reason:no_token} and the dance does not play; self.remote.session then self.remote.action {"kind":"dance"} then self.robot.dance {"source":"remote","token":<that token>} plays it; the same call with "source":"user" or no source plays with no token; self.remote.history lists the refusal.

